<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcide De Gasperi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alcide De Gasperi was an Italian statesman, founder of the Christian Democracy party, and prime minister from 1945 to 1953. He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> through the transition from monarchy to republic in 1946, anchored it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a>, and was one of the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi.jpg" alt="Alcide De Gasperi" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcide_de_Gasperi_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>De Gasperi was born on 3 April 1881 in Pieve Tesino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the son of a local police officer of limited means. He was active in the Social Christian movement from 1896 and studied literature and philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from 1900, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>'s encyclical Rerum novarum. In 1904 he was imprisoned briefly with other students after protesting for an Italian-language university. From 1906 he edited the newspaper Il Trentino, which defended Italian culture in Trentino against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a>; at that time he did not seek unification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He sat in the Austrian Reichsrat from 1911 for six years, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he sided with Italy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919 he helped found the Italian People's Party with Luigi Sturzo and served in the Italian Parliament from 1921 to 1924. He first backed the party's participation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s government but diverged from the Fascists over the Acerbo Law and violence against the constitutional parties, culminating in the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a>. The party was dissolved in November 1926. He was arrested in March 1927 and sentenced to four years in prison; the Vatican negotiated his release. From 1929 until July 1943 he worked as a cataloguer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>. In 1943 he published Ideas for Reconstruction, a programme for the Christian Democracy party, of which he became general secretary in 1944.</p><p>He became prime minister in December 1945, leading eight successive governments until 1953, at first with the Communists and Socialists. He was provisional head of state from 12 June to 28 June 1946, after a referendum in which 54 per cent voted for a republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> signed the peace treaty of 1947, which lost its eastern border area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1949 and the European Coal and Steel Community. The Gruber–De Gasperi Agreement of September 1946 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> an autonomous region. In May 1947 he formed a government without the Communists and Socialists, and in April 1948 the Christian Democrats won 48.5 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The 1953 election followed a change in the electoral law that its opponents called the Scam Law; the governing coalition won 49.9 per cent, just short of the supermajority bonus, and De Gasperi was forced to resign on 2 August. He gave up the party leadership in June 1954 and died on 19 August 1954 at Sella di Valsugana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>. He is buried in the Basilica di San Lorenzo fuori le <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mur-river">Mura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the process for his beatification was opened in 1993. His former secretary Giulio Andreotti said that he taught his followers to search for compromise and to mediate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alcide De Gasperi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Italy the Christian Democratic Party led the coalition government under <b>Alcide De Gasperi</b>, and in France the Popular Republican Movement became the largest party in parliament in 1946.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/christian-democracy">Christian democracy · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian Democrat governments led by <b>Alcide De Gasperi</b> and his successors anchored it in NATO in 1949 and the European Economic Community, founded by the Treaty of Rome in 1957.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-republic">Italian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pieve Tesino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Borgo Valsugana, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.07,11.61&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Pieve Tesino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 542 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alcide De Gasperi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.07,11.61&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
